    <region left="18" top="7406" width="740" height="32" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Flow Pattern Observations</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="7450" width="740" height="66" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Separation angle from the sketches: about 90° from the front stagnation point at every setpoint, close to the 80° expected for a laminar boundary layer. The wake grew wider and more turbulent as the setpoint increased.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="7538" width="740" height="32" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Plot</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="7582" width="740" height="30" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Chart generated in MS Excel, Lab_10_-_Cylinder_Drag_Plot.xlsx. Error bars show ± uncertainty.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="7624" width="720" height="449" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8099" width="740" height="32" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Q/A</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8143" width="740" height="84" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Flow pattern with increasing Reynolds number: at setpoint 10 the flow stayed close to the cylinder and formed a small, slow recirculation zone behind it. As the Reynolds number increased, the separated region grew into a wider, more chaotic turbulent wake with swirling vortices, and the streamlines bent farther around the cylinder.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8239" width="740" height="102" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>The separation point depends on whether the boundary layer is laminar or turbulent, which is set mainly by the Reynolds number, surface roughness, and freestream turbulence. A laminar boundary layer separates early, near 80°, which leaves a wide wake with low pressure behind the cylinder and a high drag coefficient. A turbulent boundary layer stays attached to about 140°, which narrows the wake, reduces pressure drag, and causes the sudden drop in drag coefficient known as the drag crisis.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8353" width="740" height="102" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Periodic vortex shedding happens because the two shear layers that separate from the top and bottom of the cylinder are unstable. Each layer rolls up into a vortex, and as one vortex grows it pulls the opposite shear layer across the wake and cuts itself off. The vortices then form on alternating sides, creating the von Kármán vortex street. For a cylinder, shedding begins near Re = 90. Reference: Cengel and Cimbala, Fluid Mechanics: Fundamentals and Applications, Section 15-6.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="8467" width="740" height="102" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="740" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>The Strouhal number, St = f·D/V, describes the vortex shedding frequency f. For a cylinder it stays near 0.2 over a wide range of Reynolds numbers. It is important because shedding creates oscillating forces, and if the shedding frequency matches a structure's natural frequency, resonance can cause large vibrations or failure. It is used in designing chimneys, bridges, power lines, heat exchanger tubes, offshore risers, and vortex flowmeters.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
  </regions>
</worksheet>
